  <!-- Headline -->
  <h2>What Actually Makes ByteSIM Different</h2>
  <p>
    Most eSIM apps on this site sell data and nothing else. ByteSIM sells real local phone numbers in select countries, including the US, Canada, UK, France, Spain, Australia, Vietnam, South Korea, and Thailand, bundled with plans that add calls, texts, hotspot sharing, and a local IP address on top of data. That's a genuinely different product from a data-only eSIM, and it's the reason we bought one ourselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    ByteSIM's own site is upfront that most of its travel eSIM plans don't require ID verification, but local-number plans do, per local telecom regulation. That distinction matters because it's not a blanket "hand over your passport for any eSIM" policy the way it might read at first; it's specific to actually getting a real number.
  </p>

  <!-- Real first-hand purchase writeup -->
  <h2>What Buying a Spain Number Actually Involved</h2>
  <p>
    We bought ByteSIM's Spain "Mega Plan with Unlimited Calls & EU Roaming" (SKU spain-eSIM-LBES60GB-27): 100GB of Spain data, 15GB of EU roaming data, unlimited calls within Spain, and 500 minutes of EU roaming calls, for 28 days. Listed at $13.90, it came to <strong>$13.20</strong> after ByteSIM's automatic Summer Sale discount, no code needed. The reason we picked this specific plan over a plain data-only one: it comes with a real <strong>+34 Spanish phone number</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Getting that number meant identity verification: uploading a passport photo and a selfie, standard practice for getting an actual phone number in many countries, not a ByteSIM quirk. After verification, the eSIM didn't activate right away, which had us worried for a minute. We used the "Get Help" button in WhatsApp and had a real person responding within minutes; they said to just wait 1 to 10 minutes after verification completes. It worked about 30 seconds later. ByteSIM support followed up on their own two hours after that just to check how it was going. That's a better support experience than most eSIM apps we've dealt with, bot-driven or otherwise.
  </p>

